          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Curiosity | Textbook of Science | Grade 7"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.083,
            0.884,
            0.177
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The lamp glows in the arrangements at S.No. 1 and 6 and does not glow in the remaining arrangements. Now, carefully look at the arrangements in which the lamp glows. "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Compare",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " these with those in which the lamp does not glow. Can you find the reason for the difference?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.188,
            0.584,
            0.21
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3.2.5 An electrical circuit"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.217,
            0.883,
            0.274
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The lamp glows when one terminal of the lamp is connected to one terminal of the electric cell and the other terminal of the lamp to the other terminal of the cell as shown in Fig. 3.9. This"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.293,
            0.37,
            0.514
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.158,
                0.293,
                0.37,
                0.514
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_7_image_4.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.148,
                0.52,
                0.373,
                0.537
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 3.9: An electrical circuit"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.384,
            0.273,
            0.883,
            0.347
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "setup forms an "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "electrical circuit",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", which provides a complete path for electric current to flow through the lamp. The lamp glows only when current passes through the circuit."
            }
          ],
          "continues_prev": true
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.383,
            0.348,
            0.885,
            0.553
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The direction of electric current in an electrical circuit is taken to be from the positive to the negative terminal of the electric cell. When the terminals of the lamp are connected with those of the electric cell by wires, the current passes through the filament of the incandescent lamp and makes it glow. With an incandescent lamp, it does not matter which of its terminals connects to the positive or negative terminal of the cell. The lamp will glow as long as the circuit is complete and current flows through the filament."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.561,
            0.275,
            0.688
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.175,
                0.561,
                0.275,
                0.688
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_7_image_8.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.279,
            0.582,
            0.5,
            0.601
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fascinating facts"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.277,
            0.605,
            0.854,
            0.677
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Sometimes, an incandescent lamp does not glow even when connected to a cell. We say the lamp has ‘fused’ usually due to a broken filament. A broken filament stops the flow of current, preventing the lamp from glowing."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.256,
            0.701,
            0.613,
            0.721
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us now try to make an LED glow."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.275,
            0.73,
            0.585,
            0.752
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 3.7: Let us experiment"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.267,
            0.768,
            0.883,
            0.823
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Take two electric cells, an LED of any colour, a cell holder that can fit two cells (Fig. 3.10a), and two lengths of electric wire."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.267,
            0.825,
            0.884,
            0.862
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Remove about 1 cm of the plastic covering from both ends of each wire to expose the metal."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.267,
            0.864,
            0.883,
            0.901
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Connect the two wires to the cell holder as shown in Fig. 3.10a."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 16,
